: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold)].\n', 'reasoning': "**Analysis of a River Overbet in Heads-Up Poker**\n\nOkay, so here's the situation. I'm in hand six of a twenty-hand match. We all start with 20,000 chips each hand, and I'm currently at 19,900. The history shows a check-check preflop, flop, and turn. The board is now [Jd, 9h, Jc, Qd, 4c] – a paired board with a Queen, Nine, and Four. My hand is [Ks, 2d]. That makes my best hand a pair of Jacks with a King kicker.\n\nThe opponent is all-in on the river for nearly 20,000 into a tiny pot. This is a massive overbet. Given the action so far – check-check on every street – and the board texture, this usually means one of two things: a complete monster or a desperate bluff.\n\nLet's break it down. What could they have that beats me? Any pocket pair (22 or higher), any Ace, any Queen, any Jack (trips or better), any Nine, or any Four. Any Ten + any other card gives them a straight which also beats me, or any combination of cards that makes any of these hands.\n\nWhat do I beat? Pure bluffs – hands with nothing but low cards. Not even a Ten high. I am effectively playing the board with a King kicker and that's not strong enough to call.\n\nThe odds are terrible. I'm risking my entire stack to win a pittance. To make this call profitable, I'd need to win about 50% of the time, and I am nowhere near that kind of equity. In fact, if you just compare to a random hand, my King high would lose about 75% of the time.\n\nGiven the match format, it's clear I am currently ahead and this could swing the game in either direction. Losing this hand wipes out my gains and could put me behind. I've only lost a small amount so far. Folding is the only sensible play here.\n\n**Evaluation:** Facing an all-in overbet on the river with only King-high (playing the board) is a clear fold. The hand strength is too weak to catch a bluff, and the risk/reward ratio is terrible. I am currently leading the match, and folding preserves my position while calling risks a massive loss for a negligible gain.\n\n**Reason:** The opponent has made a massive overbet on the river into a tiny pot. My hand is merely King-high (playing the board's pair of Jacks with a King kicker). This hand loses to any pair (including pocket pairs 22+), any Ace, any Queen, any Jack, any Nine, and any Four. It also loses to straights. The only hands I beat are pure bluffs with Ten-high or worse. Given the pot odds require me to be right ~50% of the time and the range of hands beating me is overwhelming, calling is a losing play. Folding minimizes loss to a trivial amount.\n\n**Answer:** -1\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: The current pot is very small (likely around 200 chips), and the opponent has made a massive overbet by going All-in (approx. 19,900 chips) on the river. To call, I would need to risk my entire stack of nearly 20,000 chips. The pot odds dictate that I would need approximately 50% equity (chance of winning) to justify this call. My hand is King-high (King of Spades, 2 of Diamonds) on a board of Jack, 9, Jack, Queen, 4. My final hand is a Pair of Jacks (from the board) with a King kicker. This is a very weak hand that loses to any pair in the opponent's hand (22+), any Ace, any Queen, any Jack, any Nine, or any Four. It also loses to any straight. I only beat a pure bluff with Ten-high or worse. Even against a completely random hand, King-high on this board loses about 70-75% of the time. Given I am currently in the lead in the overall match, there is no incentive to take such a high-risk, negative expected value variance. Folding costs a negligible amount of chips.\n\nReason: The opponent's all-in bet is a massive overbet into a small pot. My hand is effectively playing the board with a King kicker, which is very weak and loses to the vast majority of possible hands (any pair, any Ace, or any board connection). The risk of losing my entire stack far outweighs the potential gain of a small pot. Folding is the mathematically correct 